<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- The app talks to Supabase. Everything it shows comes from there.
         ===================================================================
         **This was missing, and it made release builds offline.** The Flutter
         template declares INTERNET in `src/debug/` and `src/profile/` only —
         those two exist so the tooling can attach for hot reload — and leaves
         the main manifest without it, on the assumption that the developer
         adds it when the app actually needs the network.

         Nothing catches the omission. Debug and profile builds work because
         their manifests merge the permission in, so every emulator run and
         every `flutter run` is fine. The *release* build is the only one that
         ships without it, and Android does not refuse the connection — it
         fails the DNS lookup:

           SocketException: Failed host lookup: '<project>.supabase.co'
           (OS Error: No address associated with hostname)

         Which reads as a broken phone or a broken ISP. The first real install
         was spent looking at Private DNS settings.

         No ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE: nothing here inspects the connection type, and
         `BackendHealth` answers "is the backend reachable" by asking it rather
         than by asking Android what kind of radio is on. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!-- The evening reminder has to survive a reboot (Sprint 56).
         ===================================================================
         A scheduled alarm is held by the system, not by this app, and Android
         drops every one of them when the device restarts. Without this the
         reminder works perfectly until the first reboot and then silently never
         fires again — which is the worst possible failure for a feature whose
         entire job is to be remembered on a day nobody opened the app.

         POST_NOTIFICATIONS and VIBRATE are not here on purpose: the plugin
         declares both in its own manifest and the merger brings them in.

         No SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM or USE_EXACT_ALARM either, and that is a
         deliberate trade rather than an omission. Those grant alarms that fire
         to the second, at the cost of a permission Google Play restricts to apps
         that genuinely need alarm-clock precision. "Around half five" is the
         whole requirement here, so the schedule is inexact and Android is free to
         batch it with whatever else it was going to wake up for. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <application
        android:label="What's Cooking?"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- The deep link back from an email (Sprint 52).
                 =====================================================
                 **This was missing, and it broke password reset silently.** The
                 app has been sending `whatscooking://reset-password` as the
                 `redirectTo` on every reset email since the auth sprint
                 (AppConstants.passwordResetRedirect), and nothing on either
                 platform claimed the scheme — so the link in the email opened
                 nothing at all. No error, no log: a tap that does nothing, which
                 reads as a broken email rather than a missing intent filter.

                 The scheme is registered without a host on purpose. Supabase
                 sends more than one path back — reset-password now, a
                 confirmation callback if email confirmation is turned on — and a
                 filter pinned to one host would work for that one and fail the
                 same silent way for the next.

                 `singleTop` above matters here: without it the link would start
                 a second copy of the activity on top of the running app, and the
                 session it delivers would land in the wrong one.

                 This is a custom scheme, not an App Link, so there is no domain
                 verification and no assetlinks.json to serve. The trade is that
                 any app may claim the same scheme; for a two-phone household that
                 is a better deal than owning and verifying a domain.

                 The matching allow-list entry has to exist in the Supabase
                 dashboard under Authentication → URL Configuration, or the
                 backend refuses to send the link at all. See supabase/README.md. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="whatscooking"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />

        <!-- flutter_local_notifications' own receivers (Sprint 56).
             The plugin ships the classes but not the declarations, so an app that
             forgets these compiles, installs, schedules — and shows nothing. The
             first delivers a scheduled notification; the second re-registers every
             pending alarm after a restart or an app update, which is what the
             RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED permission above is for.

             MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED matters as much as BOOT_COMPLETED here: installing
             a new APK over the old one also clears the alarms, and this household
             sideloads every build. The two QUICKBOOT actions are for the handset
             makers who send those instead of the standard one. -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
